from pathlib import Path
from runpy import run_path

g=run_path(str(Path(__file__).with_name('author-tools-generator.py')))
L=g['lesson']; C=g['course']
def P(*a): return L(*a,language='python')

C('machine-learning','Machine learning finds patterns in examples. Learn inputs, labels, simple predictions and fair checks using tiny lists of numbers that you can follow by hand.','The Python examples need a separate Python 3 environment. They use built-in tools, not a paid AI service. These small demonstrations do not make reliable real-world predictions.','https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course',[
P('Learn from examples','Machine learning uses examples to choose a rule or model. A model is a stored way to make predictions. A prediction is an estimate, not a known fact.','examples = [(1, 2), (2, 4), (3, 6)]\nfor pages, minutes in examples:\n    print(pages, minutes)','Each pair connects a page count to fictional reading minutes. The pattern suggests twice as many minutes as pages, but three examples are not proof.','Add the pair (4, 8) and explain its meaning.','Do not say that every learner reads at the same speed.','What is a prediction?','An estimate from a model','A guaranteed fact'),
P('Inputs and known answers','A feature is an input used for a prediction. A label is the known answer in a teaching example. Keep each input paired with the right answer.','features = [[1], [2], [3]]\nlabels = [2, 4, 6]\nprint(features[1], labels[1])','The output is [2] 4. The second feature row has two pages, and its label has four fictional minutes.','Find the third input and its label.','Shuffling inputs without shuffling their matching labels breaks the pairs.','What is the label for the second row?','4 minutes','2 pages'),
P('Regression predicts numbers','Regression is a task that predicts a number. Here we supply a doubling rule ourselves so the calculation is easy to inspect.','def predict_minutes(pages):\n    return 2 * pages\nprint(predict_minutes(4))','The output is 8 because 2 times 4 is 8. This rule was written by a person; no training happened in this code.','Predict for five pages, then check the calculation.','A hand-written rule is not automatically a learned model.','What kind of answer does regression predict?','A number','Only a colour name'),
P('Classification chooses a group','Classification predicts a group label. For this toy example, a threshold is the number where our choice changes.','def group(size):\n    return "small" if size < 5 else "large"\nprint(group(3), group(8))','The output is small large. The threshold is 5. A real classifier might learn a threshold from labelled examples.','Find what happens when size is exactly 5.','A class label is a choice of group; it does not show how certain that choice is.','Which value is the threshold?','5','8'),
P('Try the nearest example','A nearest-neighbour rule uses the closest stored example. Distance tells us how far two values are apart. Here distance is the size of their difference.','examples = [(1, "small"), (3, "small"), (8, "large")]\nquery = 7\nnearest = min(examples, key=lambda row: abs(row[0] - query))\nprint(nearest[1])','The output is large. Distances from 7 are 6, 4 and 1, so the example at 8 is closest. min chooses the pair with the smallest distance.','Use query 2 and notice that a tie needs a rule.','One nearby example may be noisy or wrong; it is not enough evidence for a serious decision.','Which stored size is closest to 7?','8','3'),
P('Keep a test set aside','Training examples help choose a model. Test examples are kept aside to check it later. Keeping them separate helps reveal mistakes on unseen data.','rows = [(1, 2), (2, 4), (3, 6), (4, 8)]\ntrain = rows[:3]\ntest = rows[3:]\nprint(len(train), len(test))','The output is 3 1. The first three pairs form the training list. The final pair forms the test list. This ordered split is only a small demonstration.','Explain why copying the last row into train would weaken this check.','Real splits must account for time and related records; a simple slice is not always fair.','How many rows are in the test set?','1','3'),
P('Compare with a baseline','A baseline is a simple prediction rule used for comparison. Always guessing the training average is one baseline for a number task.','training_answers = [2, 4, 6]\nbaseline = sum(training_answers) / len(training_answers)\nprint(baseline)','The output is 4.0. The baseline adds the three labels, gets 12, then divides by 3. It will guess 4 for any new input.','Calculate this baseline for labels [3, 6, 9].','A more complex model should earn its complexity by passing useful checks.','What does this baseline guess each time?','The training average','The next true answer'),
P('Measure a numeric mistake','Absolute error is the size of the difference between an answer and a prediction. It drops the minus sign so the size is always nonnegative.','answers = [4, 8]\npredictions = [5, 6]\nerrors = [abs(a - p) for a, p in zip(answers, predictions)]\nprint(errors, sum(errors) / len(errors))','The output is [1, 2] 1.5. The mistakes are one minute and two minutes. Their average is one and a half minutes.','Change the second prediction to 8 and find the new average error.','Keep the units: an error of 1.5 minutes is different from 1.5 hours.','What is the average absolute error?','1.5','3'),
P('Accuracy needs context','Accuracy is the share of group guesses that are correct. It can hide poor performance on a rare group.','answers = ["dry", "dry", "dry", "rain"]\npredictions = ["dry"] * 4\ncorrect = sum(a == p for a, p in zip(answers, predictions))\nprint(correct / len(answers))','The output is 0.75. Three of four guesses match, but the rule misses the only rain example.','Count how many rain examples were predicted correctly.','A high overall score can hide failure on the group that matters most.','How many rain examples did this rule get right?','0','1'),
P('Memorising is not generalising','Overfitting happens when a model follows training details too closely and does poorly on new examples. Generalising means working on useful new cases.','memorised = {1: 2, 2: 4, 3: 6}\nprint(memorised.get(4, "unknown"))','The output is unknown. A lookup table can repeat stored answers but has no rule here for input 4. This illustrates a limit of memorising.','Explain why perfect recall of three rows does not prove a useful prediction rule.','Do not choose a model only by its training score.','Which result best checks generalising?','Results on suitable unseen examples','Only results on memorised rows'),
P('Group without answer labels','Clustering groups similar inputs without known class labels. Here we check distance to two fixed centres; a real clustering method can learn its centres.','centres = [2, 8]\npoint = 7\ndistances = [abs(point - centre) for centre in centres]\nprint(distances.index(min(distances)))','The output is 1, the index of centre 8. Distances are 5 and 1. Index 1 means the second centre, not a truth label supplied by a teacher.','Assign point 3 to a centre by hand.','A discovered group does not explain why its members are alike.','Were answer labels used in this distance check?','No','Yes'),
L('Write an honest model report','A useful model report explains the task, data, checks and limits. Readers need enough detail to know where the result may fail.','Task: estimate reading minutes\nData: fictional page-and-time pairs\nCheck: compare held-out errors with a simple baseline\nLimit: too little data for real learners\nDecision: keep this as a classroom demonstration','The report connects a result to its limits. It does not claim the toy model knows how all children read.','Add one question you would ask before collecting any real learner data.','Keep private information out of practice data and include a human review for important decisions.','What should accompany a model score?','Its data, check method and limits','Only a claim that it is perfect'),
])

C('deep-learning','Deep learning uses layers of number calculations. Learn weights, activations, mistakes and small updates with examples you can work out before using a large library.','These Python 3 examples run in a separate Python environment and need no extra package. They teach calculations, not a useful trained AI system. Start with the machine-learning course if inputs and test sets are new.','https://d2l.ai/chapter_introduction/index.html',[
P('Start with a weighted input','A weight controls how strongly an input affects a calculation. A bias is a number added afterward. A neural network combines many such calculations.','input_value = 3\nweight = 2\nbias = 1\nprint(input_value * weight + bias)','The output is 7. First 3 times 2 makes 6. Adding the bias 1 makes 7. These numbers were chosen by us, not learned.','Change the bias to 0 and calculate the result.','The word neuron is a loose comparison; this simple calculation is not a whole brain cell.','What does the bias do here?','Adds 1 after multiplication','Changes the input to a word'),
P('Combine two features','One unit can combine several inputs. Multiply each input by its own weight, add the products, then add the bias.','inputs = [2, 3]\nweights = [4, 1]\nbias = 1\nvalue = sum(x * w for x, w in zip(inputs, weights)) + bias\nprint(value)','The output is 12. The products are 8 and 3, and the bias adds 1. zip pairs each input with its matching weight.','Change only the second weight to 2.','Each feature needs the intended matching weight; order matters.','Which two products are added?','8 and 3','2 and 4'),
P('An activation changes the shape','An activation applies a chosen rule after a weighted sum. ReLU keeps positive numbers and changes negative numbers to zero.','def relu(value):\n    return max(0, value)\nprint([relu(v) for v in [-2, 0, 3]])','The output is [0, 0, 3]. max picks the larger of zero and the input. This bend helps networks model more varied patterns.','Try the values [-1, 2, 5].','ReLU does not turn every output into a probability.','What does ReLU return for -2?','0','2'),
P('Make a small layer','A layer is a group of units that work on the same incoming values. Two units can produce two outputs from one input.','x = 3\nfirst = max(0, x * 2 + 1)\nsecond = max(0, x * -1 + 1)\nprint([first, second])','The output is [7, 0]. The first weighted sum is 7. The second is -2, so ReLU changes it to 0.','Use x equal to 0 and calculate both outputs.','Two output values do not mean two examples; here one example produced two values.','How many outputs does this layer make for one input?','2','3'),
P('Connect layers in order','A later layer uses the earlier layer outputs as its inputs. Deep networks have several layers that transform information step by step.','hidden = [7, 0]\noutput = hidden[0] * 0.5 + hidden[1] * 2\nprint(output)','The output is 3.5. The first hidden value contributes 3.5, and the second contributes zero. This is a chosen calculation, not a trained prediction.','Change the second hidden value to 1.','Without suitable activations, stacking plain linear layers still gives one overall linear rule.','What does the later layer use as input?','Earlier layer outputs','Only the file name'),
P('Use shape to check connections','Shape describes how values are arranged. For a batch, each row is one example. A layer must receive the number of features it expects.','batch = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]\nprint(len(batch), len(batch[0]))','The output is 3 2. There are three example rows, each with two features. A two-input layer can accept these rows.','Draw a batch with two examples and three features each.','Do not mix the number of examples with the number of features.','How many features are in each row?','2','3'),
P('Loss measures a mistake','A loss is a number that measures how wrong a prediction is. Squared error multiplies the difference by itself.','prediction = 5\nanswer = 3\nloss = (prediction - answer) ** 2\nprint(loss)','The output is 4. The difference is 2, and 2 squared is 4. A perfect prediction would have zero squared error.','Use prediction 2 with the same answer.','Small training loss alone does not prove good results on new data.','What is the squared error here?','4','2'),
P('A slope points toward a change','A slope describes how quickly a value changes nearby. For the loss (weight - 3) squared, its slope is twice (weight - 3).','weight = 5.0\nslope = 2 * (weight - 3)\nprint(slope)','The output is 4.0. Near weight 5, increasing the weight increases this loss. Moving a little in the other direction can reduce it.','Find the slope at weight 2.','A slope is a local guide; a large jump can go past a useful value.','Is the slope at weight 5 positive?','Yes','No'),
P('Take one learning step','The learning rate sets how big a weight update is. Subtracting a small amount in the slope direction can reduce the loss.','weight = 5.0\nrate = 0.1\nslope = 2 * (weight - 3)\nweight = weight - rate * slope\nprint(round(weight, 2))','The output is 4.6. The update subtracts 0.4 from 5. This brings the weight closer to the target 3 for this simple loss.','Try rate 0.2 and find the new weight.','A larger learning rate is not always better; it can overshoot.','How much is subtracted in this step?','0.4','4.0'),
L('Backpropagation follows the links','Backpropagation finds how each weight affects the loss by following the calculation links backward. Libraries handle this bookkeeping for large networks.','Forward: input -> hidden layer -> output -> loss\nBackward: loss -> output weights -> hidden weights\nUpdate: use each weight slope to make a small change','The forward pass produces the answer and loss. The backward pass calculates slopes. A separate update step changes the weights.','Point to the step that measures a mistake and the step that changes weights.','Calculating slopes does not itself update weights in every library.','Which direction computes the answer first?','Forward','Backward'),
L('Batches and epochs','A batch is a small group of examples processed together. An epoch is one pass through the training set. More passes do not guarantee a better test result.','Training set: 12 examples\nBatch size: 4 examples\nOne epoch: 3 batches\nTwo epochs: 6 batches in total','Twelve divided by four makes three batches per pass. Two passes make six batch visits. Each pass uses the same training examples again.','Work out batches per epoch for 20 examples and batch size 5.','Do not count an epoch as a new set of data.','How many batches make one epoch here?','3','12'),
L('Check before trusting a network','Training, validation and test sets have different jobs. Validation helps choose settings. The final test checks a choice that has already been made.','Training: adjust weights\nValidation: choose settings and when to stop\nTest: report the final check\nReport: describe errors and data limits','Keeping these jobs separate reduces the chance of tuning the model to the answers used for the final report.','Explain why repeatedly picking settings from final test scores is a problem.','A large network can learn unfair or misleading patterns from its data. Check important groups and failure cases.','Which set should guide setting choices?','Validation','The final test set over and over'),
])

C('artificial-intelligence','Artificial intelligence includes methods for search, planning and learning. Explore simple choices and checkable rules with classroom examples and clear limits.','Most lessons are worked plans you can read without installing anything. Short Python examples need a separate Python 3 environment. This app does not call an AI service or need an account.','https://ai4k12.org/resources/',[
L('What an AI task looks like','Artificial intelligence, or AI, is a broad area of computing. It includes programs that search for plans or use data to make predictions. Different tasks need different methods.','Task: help find a library book\nInput: a title typed by a learner\nOutput: matching titles\nCheck: compare with the real catalogue','This plan names a job and a way to check its result. Calling a program AI does not explain whether it does that job well.','Name the input and output for a route-planning task.','Do not assume an AI label means a program understands everything.','What should be checked first?','Whether the result fits the task','Whether the name sounds clever'),
P('Use an explicit rule','A rule tells a program exactly what to do when a condition is met. Some AI systems include rules, while others learn patterns from data.','books_left = 0\nif books_left == 0:\n    print("Ask for a return date")\nelse:\n    print("A copy is available")','The output asks for a return date because the count is zero. The condition checks equality before choosing the message.','Set books_left to 2 and follow the other branch.','A rule can only handle the cases its author considered.','Which branch runs when books_left is 0?','The first branch','The else branch'),
L('Represent a problem as states','A state is a description of the current situation. An action changes one state into another. A goal describes the state we want to reach.','Start state: at classroom\nAction: walk to corridor\nNext state: at corridor\nAction: walk to library\nGoal state: at library','The plan breaks one trip into two allowed moves. Search can look for a chain of moves from the start to the goal.','Add a reading room and one allowed move to it.','A plan must use moves that are actually allowed in the problem.','What is the goal state here?','At library','At classroom'),
P('Store connected places','A graph stores things and their connections. We can describe school rooms with a dictionary whose lists name directly connected rooms.','rooms = {"class": ["hall"], "hall": ["class", "library"], "library": ["hall"]}\nprint(rooms["hall"])','The output is [\'class\', \'library\']. These rooms are one move from the hall. The dictionary does not include travel time.','Add a garden connected to the hall in both directions.','If travel is allowed both ways, remember to record both connections.','Which room is directly connected to class?','hall','library'),
P('Search one step at a time','Breadth-first search checks nearer places before farther places. A queue holds places still waiting to be checked.','from collections import deque\nrooms = {"class": ["hall"], "hall": ["class", "library"], "library": ["hall"]}\nqueue = deque(["class"])\nseen = {"class"}\norder = []\nwhile queue:\n    room = queue.popleft()\n    order.append(room)\n    for next_room in rooms[room]:\n        if next_room not in seen:\n            seen.add(next_room)\n            queue.append(next_room)\nprint(order)','The output visits class, hall, then library. popleft takes the oldest waiting room. seen stops the search from looping back forever.','Explain why class is not added again from hall.','Breadth-first search finds fewest moves only when every move has equal cost.','What prevents repeated visits here?','The seen set','The room name length'),
L('A helpful guess can guide search','A heuristic is a useful estimate that guides search. A straight-line distance can suggest which place is nearer, but it may ignore walls or closed paths.','Route A: looks short on a map, but a gate is closed\nRoute B: looks longer, and all paths are open\nNext check: verify which moves are allowed','The estimate helps pick where to look. Checking the actual paths is still necessary before choosing a route.','Explain why a route through a wall is invalid even if its distance is small.','A helpful estimate is not the same as a proven best answer.','Does a short map distance prove a path is usable?','No','Yes'),
P('Respect all constraints','A constraint is a rule every allowed answer must satisfy. A timetable needs a free room and an available teacher at the same time.','slots = ["Monday", "Tuesday", "Wednesday"]\nroom_free = {"Monday", "Wednesday"}\nteacher_free = {"Tuesday", "Wednesday"}\nallowed = [s for s in slots if s in room_free and s in teacher_free]\nprint(allowed)','The output is [\'Wednesday\']. It is the only slot in both sets. The word and requires both conditions to be true.','Remove Wednesday from room_free and inspect the empty answer list.','An empty answer may mean the rules cannot all be met; do not silently ignore a rule.','Which slot satisfies both constraints?','Wednesday','Monday'),
L('Rules and learned patterns','A rule-based program follows rules written by people. A learned model finds patterns using examples. Both need testing and can make mistakes.','Written rule: a book is long if pages > 200\nLearned model: use labelled book examples to choose a pattern\nCheck: compare decisions with the task definition','The first method has an explicit threshold. The second depends on teaching examples and its learning method. Neither definition makes the result automatically correct.','Name one change in the data that could affect the learned pattern.','Do not call a fixed rule learned unless a learning process actually chose it.','Where do learned patterns come from?','Examples and a learning method','Only the program title'),
P('Uncertainty deserves a number','Uncertainty means we do not know the outcome for sure. A past fraction can describe observed results, but a small sample gives weak evidence about the future.','rainy_days = 3\nobserved_days = 10\nprint(rainy_days / observed_days)','The output is 0.3. Three out of ten observed days were rainy. This alone is not a weather forecast for tomorrow.','Explain how observations from one season may miss another season.','Do not turn a small observed fraction into a guaranteed future chance.','What does 0.3 describe here?','The observed fraction of rainy days','A certain forecast'),
L('Agents observe and act','An agent is a system that observes a situation and chooses an action. It needs clear allowed actions and a way to check what happened.','Observe: shelf count is zero\nSuggest: ask when a book may return\nHuman checks: is the shelf count current?\nAllowed action in this demo: show a suggestion','The system reads a count and suggests a next step. It does not send messages or alter a real library record.','Write one condition that should stop an action.','Giving a system an action tool is different from merely asking it for advice.','What action is allowed in this demo?','Show a suggestion','Change real library records'),
L('Check who the data represents','A dataset is a collection of examples. If some groups or situations are missing, a model may work unevenly. Check results for the people and conditions it will serve.','Sample: only clear photos in bright daylight\nPlanned use: photos in daylight and dim rooms\nMissing check: results on suitable dim-room examples','The planned use is wider than the sample. A daylight score alone does not show whether the system works in dim rooms.','Name another condition a photo dataset might miss.','Do not assume more examples automatically remove every bias in how data was collected.','Which condition needs another check?','Dim rooms','Only bright daylight again'),
L('Keep a person able to review','An AI result should be easy to question and correct. For important choices, people need the evidence, limits and a way to reject a poor suggestion.','Suggestion: choose route B\nEvidence: route A has a closed gate\nLimit: map was checked yesterday\nReview: confirm the gate and path are still safe','The explanation lets someone test the reason for the suggestion. If the map is old, the route may need changing.','Add a way for a user to report an incorrect map detail.','A fluent explanation can still contain false facts; check the evidence.','What makes a suggestion easier to review?','Evidence and clear limits','A claim that questions are unnecessary'),
])

C('data-science','Data science uses information to answer questions. Learn to organise, clean and compare small fictional datasets, then explain what the numbers can and cannot tell us.','Run Python examples in a separate Python 3 environment. They need only built-in libraries. Use fictional classroom data; do not collect names or private learner information for these exercises.','https://www.openintro.org/book/ims/',[
L('Begin with a clear question','Data is recorded information. Data science uses careful questions and checks to learn from it. A clear question tells us what we need to count.','Question: which book type was borrowed most this week?\nNeeded: one row per borrowing and the book type\nNot needed: learner names or home addresses\nResult: a count for each type','The question asks about borrowing events. We can answer it without recording who borrowed each book.','Write a question about book returns and list only the data it needs.','Collecting extra personal details does not make an answer better.','What should guide data collection?','The question we need to answer','A wish to collect every detail'),
P('Organise rows and columns','A row holds one record. A column holds one kind of information. A list of dictionaries can represent a small table in Python.','rows = [{"type": "story", "count": 4}, {"type": "science", "count": 2}]\nprint(rows[0]["count"])','The output is 4. rows[0] chooses the first record. ["count"] reads its count field.','Add a record for poetry with count 3.','Use the same meaning and unit for a column in every row.','What does rows[0] choose?','The first record','The count column in every record'),
P('Read a tiny CSV table','CSV stores table rows as text, often with commas between fields. A CSV reader handles fields more carefully than splitting every line yourself.','import csv\nfrom io import StringIO\ntext = "type,count\\nstory,4\\nscience,2\\n"\nrows = list(csv.DictReader(StringIO(text)))\nprint(rows[0]["count"])','The output displays 4, but its value is text. DictReader uses the header names as keys. StringIO lets the reader treat the sample string like a file.','Convert the count with int and add 1.','A value read from CSV is not automatically a number.','What type is the count read by DictReader here?','Text','An integer automatically'),
P('Use consistent units','Before comparing measurements, put them in the same units. Metres and centimetres describe length on different scales.','lengths_cm = [100, 150, 200]\nlengths_m = [value / 100 for value in lengths_cm]\nprint(lengths_m)','The output is [1.0, 1.5, 2.0]. Each centimetre value is divided by 100 because a metre contains 100 centimetres.','Convert 250 centimetres to metres.','Adding 1 metre and 50 centimetres as if they were both the same unit gives a wrong result.','Why divide by 100 here?','There are 100 centimetres in a metre','There are 100 records'),
P('Handle missing values honestly','A missing value means a measurement is absent. It does not mean zero. Keep track of what you leave out of a calculation.','minutes = [10, None, 20]\nknown = [value for value in minutes if value is not None]\nprint(sum(known) / len(known), len(known))','The output is 15.0 2. The average uses only two known values. We must report that one value was missing.','Count the missing values separately.','If no values are known, check before dividing by the list length.','Should None be treated as zero automatically?','No','Yes'),
P('Check duplicate records','A duplicate is a repeated record. Some repeats are mistakes, but repeated events can also be real. Use a suitable identifier before removing anything.','events = [{"id": "a", "type": "story"}, {"id": "a", "type": "story"}, {"id": "b", "type": "story"}]\nids = [row["id"] for row in events]\nprint(len(ids), len(set(ids)))','The output is 3 2. There are three rows and two distinct event IDs. This suggests a repeated event ID worth checking.','Explain why two different story borrowings should not be merged just because their type matches.','Do not remove all repeated values when separate events can share them.','What needs review in this example?','The repeated event ID a','Every story borrowing'),
P('Count categories','A category is a named group. Counter counts how often each value occurs in a list.','from collections import Counter\nborrowings = ["story", "science", "story", "poetry"]\ncounts = Counter(borrowings)\nprint(counts["story"], counts["science"])','The output is 2 1. Story appears twice and science once. This counts events, not the number of different books available.','Add one science borrowing and count again.','Always say what a count refers to: books, events and people are different things.','How many story borrowing events are listed?','2','4'),
P('Mean and median show different centres','The mean adds values and divides by their count. The median is the middle value after sorting. A very large value can pull the mean upward.','from statistics import mean, median\nminutes = [2, 3, 4, 5, 36]\nprint(mean(minutes), median(minutes))','The output is 10 4. The sum is 50 across five values. The middle sorted value is 4. The value 36 pulls the mean far above the median.','Replace 36 with 6 and compare both measures.','Do not report an average without checking how spread out the values are.','Which measure is 4 here?','Median','Mean'),
P('Look at spread','Spread describes how far apart values are. The range subtracts the smallest value from the largest. It is a simple start, not a full description.','scores = [6, 7, 7, 10]\nprint(max(scores) - min(scores))','The output is 4 because 10 minus 6 is 4. This shows the distance between the extremes.','Compare with [6, 6, 10, 10], which has the same range.','Two lists can have the same range and very different middle values.','What does the range use?','Largest and smallest values','Only the first value'),
L('Choose a clear chart','A chart should fit the question. Bars compare category counts. A line can show ordered measurements over time. Labels must name the values and units.','Question: compare borrowings by book type\nChart: bars\nHorizontal labels: story, science, poetry\nVertical label: number of borrowings\nBar baseline: zero','The bar lengths show the counts. Starting bars at zero helps keep the visual size comparison fair.','Write a title that includes the week being measured.','A chart with missing units or a misleading scale can suggest the wrong story.','Which chart suits separate category counts?','A bar chart','An unlabeled map'),
L('A pattern does not prove a cause','Two things can change together without one causing the other. A third factor or the way data was collected may explain the pattern.','Observed: more ice cream sales and more swimming on hot days\nPossible shared cause: warm weather\nUnsupported claim: buying ice cream makes people swim','The observation shows a pattern. It does not tell us that one activity caused the other. We need a suitable study to support a cause claim.','Name a factor that could affect both umbrella sales and wet roads.','Do not turn a shared pattern into a cause claim without evidence.','What can explain both changes here?','Warm weather','A proven effect of ice cream'),
L('Report the answer and its limits','A useful report states the question, result, method and limits. Readers should be able to check where the answer came from.','Question: which type was borrowed most this week?\nSample: 4 fictional borrowing events\nResult: story had 2 events; science and poetry had 1 each\nLimit: one tiny week does not describe the whole year','The report answers its own question without stretching the evidence. The event count makes the small sample clear.','Add a sentence explaining what data could help compare several weeks.','Do not hide missing data or small samples behind a confident title.','What limit should this report mention?','Only four events from one week','That it proves every future week'),
])
